        tags: ['resistance', 'valariya', 'irane', 'aevum', 'house-kazemi', 'house-ardent', 'six-earth-children', 'founding-houses', 'grand-table'],
        content: `The resistance was not organized. It converged.

Irane did not build it. Aevum did not lead it. It found itself across two decades of careful, incremental contact conducted in the knowledge that the Arke Twins' surveillance network covered every major population center on Terra. The resistance's first advantage was that it had no center. There was no meeting hall to raid, no leader to identify. There were seven people — Irane, Aevum, and the six Earth children — who moved toward the same conclusion independently and arrived at the same table by different roads.

The six Earth children — Lyra, Mara, and Sael Wov; Seth, Dain, and Noa Osiro — had lived among humans for generations, keeping their lineage quiet. They were not a unified group. They knew of each other through Gaia and Cronus's sub-channel signals, maintained distant contact, and had never acted collectively because there had been no moment to act toward. The Arke Twins' arrival in Terra, and the emergence of Irane from the facility, gave them that moment. Irane, traveling with Aevum (then operating as a Vane-adjacent archivist of Primal records under his constructed identity as House Ardent), encountered the six lineage-holders over roughly twenty years. Each meeting confirmed the same thing: the Duty covenant was real, the weapons existed, and the Twins were violating the terms of the original accord by which they were permitted to operate on Terra at all.

**Aevum's Double Game:**
While maintaining contact with the resistance, Aevum continued reporting to the Arke Twins. He told them Irane was a failed experiment with delusions of relevance. He told them the six lineage-holders were dormant and unlikely to unify. He reported enough to remain credible and withheld enough to prevent a response. The twins believed him because his track record was perfect and because the alternative — that their oldest surviving agent had turned against them — required an admission they were not structurally capable of making. Aevum's ego had told him for centuries that he was the most important variable in any equation he entered. In this one case, his ego was correct.

**The Founding of Valariya:**
What they built was not a civilization. It was a preparation. They needed structures that would survive the sealing — bloodlines locked to weapons, governance frameworks that would function without any of the founders present, and an institutional logic built around maintaining sealed entities rather than exploiting them. The city-nation of Valariya was the result. What the world saw as a founding was, from the founders' perspective, a staging ground.

The seven founding houses of Valariya:
- **House Wov** — Lyra, Mara, and Sael's line. Keepers of the Duty covenant for Gaia. Holders of the Tree of Eden.
- **Clan Osiro** — Seth, Dain, and Noa's line. Keepers of the Duty covenant for Cronus. Holders of the Book of Time.
- **House Lucerne** — Azen's line, freed from soul-chains at the moment of sealing. Holders of Morningfall (Vraka Arke sealed within).
- **House Seraph** — Elorah's line, freed from soul-chains at the moment of sealing. Holders of Covenant Seraph (Selis Arke sealed within).
- **Clan More** — the oldest Primal-era human family on Terra, already transformed by the founding period (see 8.4d). They joined as equals, not subordinates.
- **House Ardent** — Aevum's adopted cover identity. The world recorded it as a founding house. It was a single person who had lifted an existing minor house and operated behind it. What Aevum did not know: the house's bloodline traced to Darkki Ardent — his own grandson through Dulla, born in a secret no one had told him. He chose his dead son's legacy as his disguise and never found out.
- **House Kazemi** — the seventh house. Never publicly registered at the founding. Irane's identity was known to the other six founders and kept from the world entirely. Kazemi appeared in the internal covenant only — a name behind the institution, never before it. The secrecy was Irane's own decision: a governing structure built around a visible Emperor was a target. A governing structure built around an invisible covenant was a system.

The knowledge gap this created is the founding wound of Valariya. The six public houses know the structure. Only those with access to the full internal covenant know why it was built, by whom, and what it was designed to hold.`,
