        content: `**Arai's Journal — Entry I65**
*Subject Reference: No. 888,888,888*

---

She had done this enough times to make it quiet.

The notes came without thought now — height, approximate weight, the kind of body a man had when he had spent his life lifting rather than being lifted. African descent. Early twenties. A face that had not yet learned to look like anything particular. He did not have the face of a man who had already decided what he was. Some of them came in that way — mouths set, jaws forward, performing something even at the point of intake. This one had not bothered.

She had heard, from the intake staff, that he had helped the elders in the transport. That he had been considerate to the women in the holding room. The word they used was *kind*.

She wrote it down.

*"He was kind to women."*

She looked at what she had written and set the pen down.

"I wonder," she said to no one — not to him, he was not worth the words yet — "what you thought you would get from it."

She was not cruel. She was tired. There was a difference, and she had stopped being able to tell it most days. Kindness in this place was the same as kindness anywhere: a man doing small things because it cost him nothing and made him feel like something more than what he was. An animal with better manners. She had processed enough of them to know the manners didn't last. The first death took care of that.

She picked up the pen. Completed the entry with the efficiency she had spent years cultivating.

---

**SUBJECT LOG — No. 888,888,888**
Sex: Male
Origin: African Descent
Age: Early twenties
Distinguishing notes: *"He was kind to women."*

---

This is the complete record.

There are no photographs from this intake period that survived with attribution. No family name on record. No hometown. No nationality in any human categorization system. Whatever the man who entered the facility had been — whoever had helped the elders in the transport, whoever had moved carefully around the women in the holding room, whoever had looked at the world with a face that had not yet decided what it was — he left almost nothing behind.

What he left was this.

A three-line log. A woman's tired handwriting. One observation from an intake worker who had already forgotten him before the door closed.

*Entry I65* is the only existing document that describes the man before he became **Irane Kazemi** — the Emperor of Valariya, the Core of the Apex Empire, the only being in recorded history to survive what the facility called Project Unix.

She wrote it in under two minutes.

She moved on to the next file.`,
