        id: 'governing-structure',
        title: 'The 17+1 Governing Structure',
        tags: ['grand-table', 'territories', 'right-of-choice', 'king', 'authority', 'auris', '17-territories', 'throne-realm'],
        content: `The Unix World's governance was designed by Auris and repurposed by Irane. Its architecture:

**The 17 Territories:**
Each of the 17 Noble Treasure holders (excluding the Spear of Unix) governs a territory. The 14 standard families each govern one; House Wov, House Osiro, and House Kazemi each govern one. Territories are not strictly geographic — they are defined by the people who live within them.

**The Right of Choice:**
Every citizen of the Sol-Nexus chooses, at adulthood, which territory's authority they will live under for their lifetime. They select which of the 17 tools' power they submit to — and in exchange, that table member's authority protects them, governs them, and represents them to the King. This choice can be changed under specific documented circumstances, but is not made lightly.

**The 18th Realm — The Throne:**
Where all 17 territories intersect. The Spear of Unix's wielder governs from this convergence point. The King has no authority over citizens directly — only authority over the 17 table members, who each govern their chosen populations. A King who wishes to act against a citizen must go through that citizen's table member.

**The Coronation Ritual:**
To be formally crowned King and safely access the Spear's full power, the Spear's holder must be acknowledged by all 17 other tool holders simultaneously. Until that ritual is completed, the Spear's full power cannot be used safely. Niro Vane — in the 130-Year Age of Chaos — held 14 of 17 but could never complete the ritual without Wov, Osiro, and Kazemi.

**Auris's Original Design vs Irane's Repurposing:**
Auris built this system so that the Spear-holder would be absolute sovereign — a meta-king over all 17, with citizens reachable through every table member simultaneously. A perfect architecture for a single person to control the entire world without appearing to do so directly. Irane kept the structure but added the Terminus as a permanent check: the Emperor can always depose the King, and the Grand Table covenant prevents any single house from accumulating the unchecked power Niro reached for.`,
