        tags: ['vraka', 'selis', 'mana', 'core-mechanics', 'primordial', 'facility-arc-part-4'],
        content: `Vraka and Selis are, in the strictest sense, Primals — born of an Orian and a Primal, which is itself proof that Orians and Primals are the same race wearing two different outcomes of the same cores. What sets them apart is a connection to all fifteen elemental pillars at once, letting each of them use and alter Aura and Force directly rather than through a single element:

Vraka: Fire– Water– Earth– Air– Ice– Electricity– Nature– Energy– Light– Darkness– Time– Space– Life/Death; Chaotic-Darkness.
Selis: Fire– Water– Earth– Air– Ice– Electricity– Nature– Energy– Light– Darkness– Time– Space– Life/Death; Harmonic-Light.

By any ordinary measure this should be impossible — and in the grand scheme, it isn't even the most impossible thing in the room. The Primordial of Infinity, Kazemi, was pure mana and could use every element that exists; all three original Primordials could. They simply had no cores — Mana gave them cores only on reincarnating them, and Kazemi's core came out incomplete, needing other cores to complete it, a direct consequence of its destruction aspect. Vraka and Selis carrying all fifteen pillars in a single core each is a distant, survivable echo of that same original completeness — rare to the point of near-impossibility, but not a structural break in how the world works the way a true Conceptual is.

Mana himself is the actual impossible case: a being with only a harmonic-chaos core — harmony and chaos being the two base components of Aura and Force themselves — meaning his power was never a “core technique” at all. Every spell that has ever been cast by anyone, anywhere, is Mana's own nature expressing itself through others; to be a caster is to carry a share of his dominion, which is also why mana levels describe both a person's core and their place in society at once. He could kill Primordials without effort because for him it cost nothing — it was simply him, being. Reincarnating his own wife broke the one rule even he was bound by, and it is why he now sits sealed within the Pillars themselves, dead and unable to stay that way.

Arai's seal, built from Suu's original design for containing Primals, was never rated for a true Conceptual — it could not have held Irane regardless of what she believed about her own role in building it. What did hold, and hold him specifically, was never the seal's strength. It was the command already written into his own core before Arai ever touched him: Live, Kazemi. That is what a Conceptual is, underneath everything else — the will to live, given a body built to never stop until an order is fulfilled.`,
