        content: `A core is a vessel — it holds a person's life force, death force, and the consciousness bound to both, and it cannot be destroyed any more than mana itself can. When a woman conceives, a proto-core begins forming inside the child and must be fed directly from the mother's own core mana to mature; this is also why the female body can naturally sustain at most three children in a lifetime, with a fourth being a genuine, documented rarity. A proto-core takes roughly a month to become a proper one under ordinary conditions.

None of Irane's children follow the ordinary rule, and not in the same way twice. Summari and Nighla's cores, growing in Minia, arrived already fully formed rather than as proto-cores, simply developing bodies around a structure that was already complete — which is also why their forced five-year extraction left them clinically dead rather than merely premature. Urial's conception, by contrast, ran close enough to ordinary that Valariya's own rare core became the actual danger: her Life/Death-Nature-Fire, chaotic-darkness core and the Tree of Yggdrasil hybrid built to stabilize it could not anchor two cores — her own and her unborn son's — at once, and the infant's presence began burning her life force directly. Irane's answer was not to override the rule but to extend her life exactly as far as feeding a new core to full term required, and no further.

This is also the direct origin of a rule that will matter increasingly as Irane's own children come of age: a core can be transferred between bodies, dragged out of one and kept alive and functioning inside another, but only by something that already operates outside the rule entirely. Irane can do this. No one else in the world's power systems can.`,
